1
00:00:00,551 --> 00:00:02,985
Engineering needs a headcount dashboard.

2
00:00:02,985 --> 00:00:08,825
A coding agent uses the company directory, department
budgets, and HR salaries.

3
00:00:08,825 --> 00:00:11,258
It can read them all.

4
00:00:11,258 --> 00:00:12,718
But Engineering cannot.

5
00:00:14,059 --> 00:00:19,546
Private information can travel through a summary, a chart,
or a code change.

6
00:00:19,546 --> 00:00:25,033
The original file may stay private while its output
crosses into another department.

7
00:00:25,033 --> 00:00:28,409
Passing tests does not fix that access problem.

8
00:00:29,695 --> 00:00:31,727
TrustGraph checks work before publishing.

9
00:00:31,727 --> 00:00:36,603
It follows where information came from, checks who can
access the result,

10
00:00:36,603 --> 00:00:41,073
and requires a human to approve the exact version being
published.

11
00:00:42,432 --> 00:00:46,911
Connect your coding agent through MCP or the local API.

12
00:00:46,911 --> 00:00:50,047
It submits a task the operator allows.

13
00:00:50,047 --> 00:00:56,317
TrustGraph starts a fresh Codex run and records the
supplied data, instructions, and output.

14
00:00:57,824 --> 00:01:05,407
The company writes its rules in YAML: salaries are HR
only; shared planning data allows Engineering.

15
00:01:05,407 --> 00:01:07,777
Rust follows every recorded input.

16
00:01:07,777 --> 00:01:13,938
The output is accessible only to people allowed to access
every contributing source.

17
00:01:15,389 --> 00:01:17,570
This dashboard used salary data.

18
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:21,060
Engineering cannot access it, so publishing is blocked.

19
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:27,603
TrustGraph shows why: this salary file went into this
agent run, which produced this dashboard.

20
00:01:28,926 --> 00:01:33,794
Start a fresh run using the directory and separately
shared planning data.

21
00:01:33,794 --> 00:01:36,228
Exclude salaries and the restricted draft.

22
00:01:36,228 --> 00:01:39,473
The new dashboard has a new source history.

23
00:01:39,473 --> 00:01:42,718
Rewording the old draft would keep its restrictions.

24
00:01:44,096 --> 00:01:47,251
Test proposed policy changes before applying them.

25
00:01:47,251 --> 00:01:53,111
A twenty-minute-old approval passes a thirty-minute limit,
but expires under a fifteen-minute limit.

26
00:01:53,111 --> 00:01:58,069
Repeat with the same data, rules and time: the same
decision.

27
00:01:58,069 --> 00:01:59,421
Nothing is published.

28
00:02:00,835 --> 00:02:04,237
Access is allowed, but approval is still required.

29
00:02:04,237 --> 00:02:06,363
A human inspects the output.

30
00:02:06,363 --> 00:02:11,466
Approval covers that exact version, the department with
access, and the policy.

31
00:02:11,466 --> 00:02:14,442
The agent cannot approve its own work.

32
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:18,753
Edit the dashboard and its fingerprint changes.

33
00:02:18,753 --> 00:02:21,220
The old approval no longer fits.

34
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:24,509
Publishing pauses until the new version is reviewed.

35
00:02:24,509 --> 00:02:29,854
An earlier release stays in history; it does not authorize
the edited version.

36
00:02:31,305 --> 00:02:34,741
HydraDB stores the evidence; Rust checks the rules.

37
00:02:34,741 --> 00:02:41,614
The publishing gate checks again and records the attempt
before creating a GitHub draft pull request.

38
00:02:41,614 --> 00:02:45,051
An uncertain result is checked before any retry.

39
00:02:46,428 --> 00:02:49,100
Workforce dashboards, pricing tools, incident fixes.

40
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:53,551
Agents can help across departments while permissions
follow the information.

41
00:02:53,551 --> 00:02:59,339
TrustGraph explains the path from the data used to the
exact work approved.
